<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::market_volatility" target="global_market_turbulence::tech_equity_valuation" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharp volatility spikes tied to sudden US-China tech restrictions or delisting risks force margin calls, liquidity-driven selling by quant and leveraged funds, and repricing of concentrated growth exposures, directly depressing technology equity valuations.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_market_turbulence</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::market_volatility" target="global_market_turbulence::investor_risk_appetite" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Episodes of elevated financial-market volatility linked to escalations in the US–China tech competition increase realized losses and uncertainty for institutional allocators and retail investors, prompting immediate de-risking and a measurable drop in aggregate investor risk appetite.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_market_turbulence</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::market_volatility" target="russia_ukraine_war::policy_rate_expect" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in cross‑border market volatility originating from US–China tech conflict increase perceived tail‑risk and tighten financial conditions, causing central banks to push out expected timing of rate cuts or signal higher‑for‑longer policy rates, raising policy‑rate expectations.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
