<edge source="global_market_turbulence::portfolio_reallocation_r" target="state_crypto_cybercrime::cross_border_capital" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in portfolio reallocation drive rapid purchases and sales of foreign assets (FX, equities, bonds) as managers and allocators shift exposures, producing observable increases in cross-border capital flows.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_market_turbulence</data>
  <data key="d40">state_crypto_cybercrime</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_market_turbulence::portfolio_reallocation_r" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::election_policy_bias" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Elevated, rapid portfolio reallocation that produces visible asset-price shocks and revenue swings increases political pressure on incumbent governments to adopt short-term, election-friendly fiscal or regulatory measures.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_market_turbulence</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_market_turbulence::portfolio_reallocation_r" target="us_iran_standoff::geopolitical_tension_lev" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A rapid reallocation of portfolios away from Middle Eastern assets (higher reallocation rate) withdraws external capital and narrows financial options for Iran and regional proxies, increasing economic strain and the likelihood of coercive actions that elevate US–Iran tensions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_market_turbulence</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_standoff</data>
</edge>
