  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in cyberattacks concentrated on specific sectors (finance, payments, cloud) prompt institutional managers and risk systems to shift allocations away from impacted assets into cash, hedges or unaffected sectors, increasing the rate of portfolio reallocation.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">state_crypto_cybercrime</data>
  <data key="d40">global_market_turbulence</data>
</edge>
<edge source="state_crypto_cybercrime::cyberattack_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::oil_supply_demand_gap" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in successful cyberattacks on energy infrastructure (pipelines, terminals, refineries) in the Russia–Ukraine theater directly reduces effective oil throughput or forces temporary shutdowns, widening the near-term supply‑demand gap.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">state_crypto_cybercrime</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="state_crypto_cybercrime::cyberattack_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::market_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased incidence of disruptive cyber intrusions against exchanges, payment rails or clearing systems during the conflict causes trading halts, liquidity shocks and rapid repricing, directly raising financial‑market volatility.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">state_crypto_cybercrime</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
